  "warnings": [],
  "atoms": [
    {
      "id": "atom-1",
      "text": "It makes no sense for oil to be cheaper in Nigeria than in Saudi Arabia.",
      "sourceText": "It makes no sense for oil to be cheaper in Nigeria than in Saudi Arabia.",
      "start": 0,
      "end": 72,
      "role": "CORE"
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "atomId": "atom-1",
      "spans": [
        {
          "id": "doc-07b5051e0f4b::sentence-72",
          "documentId": "doc-07b5051e0f4b",
          "text": "No export or import prices are necessary. ",
          "start": 8549,
          "end": 8591,
          "context": "Real GDI is calculated using a deflator for C, I, G and Inventories.  No export or import prices are necessary.  The level comparison is based only on the relative prices of goods and services sold on domestic markets.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-4480543c9460::sentence-24",
          "documentId": "doc-4480543c9460",
          "text": "GDP per capita in Nigeria and other countries is calculated as the Gross Domestic Product (GDP) divided by the population.\n",
          "start": 890,
          "end": 1013,
          "context": "Historical series\n GDP per capita in Nigeria and other countries is calculated as the Gross Domestic Product (GDP) divided by the population.\n We show the GDP per capita in Purchasing Power Parity (PPP) terms, i.e. we calculate the GDP per capita in different countries using U.S. prices. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-07b5051e0f4b::sentence-32",
          "documentId": "doc-07b5051e0f4b",
          "text": "But in a world where trade occurs, prices that are made up of commodities produced need not correspond to the prices of commodities consumed.\n",
          "start": 2744,
          "end": 2886,
          "context": "In a world without trade between countries, the purchasing power and producing power parities will be identical, since then the commodities that a nation produced would be those that the country consumed.  But in a world where trade occurs, prices that are made up of commodities produced need not correspond to the prices of commodities consumed.\n In this note, we examine the differences in the purchasing power and producing power parity measures for Canada relative to the United States, elaborate on differences between the two, and discuss problems of estimation. "
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-4480543c9460::sentence-15",
          "documentId": "doc-4480543c9460",
          "text": "Historically, the average for Nigeria from 1990 to 2024 is 6754 U.S. dollars. ",
          "start": 486,
          "end": 564,
          "context": "In comparison, the world average is 27942 U.S. dollars, based on data from 180 countries.  Historically, the average for Nigeria from 1990 to 2024 is 6754 U.S. dollars.  The minimum value, 4782 U.S. dollars, was reached in 1999 while the maximum of 8892 U.S. dollars was recorded in 2015.\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-4480543c9460::sentence-1",
          "documentId": "doc-4480543c9460",
          "text": "Nigeria: GDP per capita, PPP\n",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 29,
          "context": "Nigeria: GDP per capita, PPP\n * indicates monthly or quarterly data series\n"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-07b5051e0f4b::sentence-113",
          "documentId": "doc-07b5051e0f4b",
          "text": "The OECD then requires producing power deflators, not purchasing power deflators. ",
          "start": 15158,
          "end": 15240,
          "context": "The Methodological Manual on Purchasing Power Parities, produced jointly by Eurostat and the Organization of Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD 2006, p. 26) stresses that the purpose of creating deflators (what it refers to as purchasing power deflators) for intercountry studies is to produce statistics that can compare \"…differences in the volumes of goods and services produced [italics added] in countries. As such, they are real measures and measures of volume.\"  The OECD then requires producing power deflators, not purchasing power deflators.  Reinforcing this, the Manual (p. 33) stresses that the measures are meant \"to make spatial volume comparisons of GDP (size of economies), GDP per head (economic welfare), and GDP per hour worked (labour productivity).\" "
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
